     + 'firewall rule, and most of them will never come near this house. But the first one that does '
     + 'turn up in a log is kept out for good there and then, because that is two things rather than '
     + 'one: somebody else judged it bad, and it has now knocked on this door. Off, and a listed '
     + 'address is only weighed more heavily than a stranger.'],
    ['feeds_weight', 'A listed address weighs (times) as much',
     'How much heavier everything a listed address does counts. Three as it comes, which means it '
     + 'reaches the bar on the third thing it tries rather than the ninth.'],
    ['geo', 'Say which country an address is in',
     'A free list of which addresses belong to which country, fetched once a month and kept here. It '
     + 'is only ever used to put a flag beside an address \u2014 nothing is judged by it, because a '
     + 'country is not something an address chooses, and a scanner in Rotterdam looks exactly like a '
     + 'phone in Rotterdam. About three hundred thousand ranges, a few megabytes.'],
    ['geo_fetch_now', 'The country list itself',
     'How many ranges are held and when they were last fetched, and a button that fetches them now '
     + 'rather than waiting for the month to come round. The list is fetched whole and replaces what '
     + 'was held, so pressing it twice costs nothing but the download.'],
    ['geo_every_d', 'Fetch the country list again every (days)',
     'The list is published once a month, so a month is the sensible answer. It is fetched whole and '
     + 'replaces what was held.'],
    ['|', 'what others know about an address'],
    ['intel_shodan', 'Ask Shodan about an address',
     'Shodan scans the whole internet and keeps what every address has open: ports, names, the '
     + 'software it answers with, known holes, and tags such as <i>scanner</i> or <i>tor</i>. On, an '
     + 'address kept out or pressing a link gets a \ud83d\udd0e button that asks it. Without a key this is '
     + 'InternetDB \u2014 free, no account, refreshed weekly; with a key, the full record. Only the '
     + 'address is sent, and never one of this house\u2019s own or one under Never kept out.'],
    ['intel_shodan_key', 'Shodan: API key (optional)',
     'From your account at shodan.io. With it, the answer also says who owns the network, the system '
     + 'it runs and when Shodan last saw it; without it InternetDB is used. Kept by the daemon, never '
     + 'sent to this page, never written to the log.'],
    ['intel_on_block', 'Ask Shodan about every address kept out',
     'The moment an address is kept out, Shodan is asked about it in the background, and the answer '
     + 'is shown on its line in the list. Two at a time at most; a burst of blocks is asked about when '
     + 'somebody looks. Off, nothing is asked until the button is pressed.'],
    ['intel_days', 'Keep an answer for (days)',
     'How long what Shodan said is kept before it is asked again. InternetDB changes once a week, so '
     + 'a week is the sensible answer.'],
    ['intel_talos', 'Offer Cisco Talos for an address',
     'A Talos button on every address kept out and every press, opening what Talos says about it on '
     + 'its own site. Talos has no public way to be asked from here; its judgement comes in through '
     + 'the <i>Cisco Talos IP block list</i> under the lists above, which says whether it names the address.'],
    ['feed_talos_accept', 'I accept Cisco\u2019s terms for the Talos list',
     'Since September 2024 snort.org hands out the Talos list only after its terms are accepted with a button, '
     + 'and every fetch comes back as the terms page instead. Agreeing to them is yours to do, not the '
     + 'daemon\u2019s: read them at <code>snort.org/downloads/ip-block-list/terms</code>, and with this on the '
     + 'daemon presses Accept in your name and fetches the list by itself, like every other list. Cisco call it '
     + 'a list for testing a Snort installation, and forbid passing it on or selling it.'],
    ['feed_talos_url', 'Where the Talos list comes from',
     'Empty: snort.org, where Talos keeps it since September 2024 \u2014 with the switch beside this one on, '
     + 'that works by itself. Only if snort.org refuses even then: accept the terms in a browser, keep the file '
     + 'on this machine (a daily wget from a cron job will do) and put its path here, e.g. '
     + '<code>/var/lib/zigmon/talos.txt</code>. An address starting with https:// is fetched instead.'],
    ['|', 'what is kept, and what is asked of it'],
    ['proxy_lines_days', 'Keep what the logs said for (days)',
     'Every line read off the server in front is kept here as a row, so questions can be asked of it '
     + 'afterwards that no single line can answer. Streaming past a line can only ask what that one '
     + 'line is worth; a table can ask how many different paths an address wanted in an hour, how long '
     + 'between its tries, and what it did across all the logs in order. 0 keeps none of them, and the '
     + 'weighing falls back to one line at a time.'],
    ['proxy_spread_bar', 'A sweep is (paths) in an hour',
     'How many different paths an address may ask for in an hour before it counts as working through '
     + 'a list rather than visiting. One path asked for a hundred times is a watch with a stale '
     + 'shortcut and weighs almost nothing; a hundred different ones is somebody looking for a way in, '
     + 'and every one of those lines looks ordinary on its own \u2014 which is exactly why reading them '
     + 'one at a time caught so little.'],
    ['|', 'the house, and who is never kept out'],
    ['block_private', 'What to do about the house\u2019s own addresses. Never: a phone on the sofa with an old shortcut can go on refusing for ever and nothing happens. Gently: three times as much has to happen, and the block always runs out, so the family is never shut out for good. Same: treated like anybody else'],
    ['trusted_proxies', 'Servers in front whose word is taken',
     'Addresses that are allowed to say who they are speaking for. A server in front of this one \u2014 '
     + 'the nginx that faces the internet \u2014 passes the presser\u2019s address along, and it is '
     + 'believed only from an address written here. Anything else saying the same thing is counted as '
     + 'itself, which is what stops somebody claiming to be a phone on the sofa.'],
    ['never_block', 'Never kept out',
     'Addresses that are never kept out, whatever else says otherwise \u2014 one each or whole ranges, of either family, comma separated, e.g. 192.168.1.0/24, 2a01:430::/48, 89.24.9.9. It beats a block made by hand as surely as one made by itself: an address here stays on the list to be seen, does nothing at all, and is never given to the router'],
  ]],
  ['The server that faces the internet', [
    ['|', 'the machine'],
    ['proxy_host', 'The address of the nginx in front, over your own link \u2014 its log sees what the public tries and gives up on before any of it reaches this machine, and none of that was ever weighed. Empty means there is none'],
    ['proxy_port', 'Its SSH port'],
    ['proxy_user', 'The user this logs in as. It only ever reads one file: give it no more than that'],
    ['proxy_auth', 'How it logs in'],
    ['proxy_password', 'The password, when it logs in with one'],
    ['|', 'the one-tap road\u2019s logs'],
    ['proxy_log', 'The full path of the access log to read, e.g. /var/log/nginx/zigmon-tap.log. Nothing else on that machine is read or written'],
    ['proxy_error_log', 'And its error log',
     'nginx writes a second log about the callers it could not even get a request from: a handshake '
     + 'that failed, a record it could not read, plain HTTP sent to the TLS port. A browser fails one '
     + 'of those once on a bad line; an address that fails one every few minutes is working through a '
     + 'list of things to try, and is worth weighing. The lines about this house\u2019s own server '
     + 'being slow to answer are nobody\u2019s fault but ours and are left alone. Usually '
     + '/var/log/nginx/zigmon-tap-error.log. Leave it empty and only the access log is read.'],
    ['|', 'the notification server\u2019s nginx logs'],
    ['ntfy_watch', 'Read the notification server\u2019s logs too',
     'Where ntfy is published by the same nginx, its two logs are read as well. They are weighed more '
     + 'carefully than the one-tap road\u2019s: a 404 there is very often a browser \u2014 with the web '
     + 'app switched off, opening the address asks for /config.js, /sw.js and the manifest and gets a '
     + 'four hundred for each \u2014 and a 403 is usually a client of your own asking for something it '
     + 'is not allowed, a phone with read-only access trying to publish. Neither is weighed. What is '
     + 'weighed is a 401: a request carrying no name the server would take. Six of those earn a block; '
     + 'forty of your own browser\u2019s four hundreds earn nothing.'],
    ['ntfy_log', 'Its access log',
     'The full path of the access log nginx writes for the ntfy name, e.g. /var/log/nginx/ntfy.log. '
     + 'Empty and nothing of it is read.'],
    ['ntfy_error_log', 'And its error log',
     'The other one, e.g. /var/log/nginx/ntfy-error.log \u2014 handshakes that failed, records that '
     + 'could not be read, plain HTTP sent to the TLS port. Weighed exactly as the one-tap road\u2019s '
     + 'error log is, and the lines about this house\u2019s own servers being slow are left alone.'],
    ['|', 'the full log, body and headers'],
    ['proxy_full_watch', 'Read the full log as well',
     'nginx can write one line of JSON per request at the one-tap road, carrying the body, every '
     + 'header worth naming, the times and the sizes \u2014 see log_format tapfull in '
     + 'deploy/nginx-tap-front.conf. It shows what an ordinary log cannot: a POST carrying no secret '
     + 'at all, a body or a content-type that road never takes, a header whose only purpose is to '
     + 'send the request somewhere else, and a body trying to be code. The link\u2019s own secret '
     + 'and the second-factor code are NOT in it \u2014 the log says whether each was there and '
     + 'nothing more, because this file sits on the machine the internet is knocking at and is '
     + 'copied here as well.'],
    ['proxy_full_log', 'Where it is',
     'The full path, e.g. /var/log/nginx/zigmon-tap-full.log. It holds bodies, so rotate it and keep '
     + 'it to root: a body is whatever somebody felt like sending. Empty and nothing of it is read.'],
    ['ntfy_full_log', 'And the notification server\u2019s',
     'The same kind of line for the ntfy name, e.g. /var/log/nginx/ntfy-full.log \u2014 see '
     + 'log_format ntfyfull in deploy/nginx-ntfy-front.conf. Read by the same rules where they make '
     + 'sense: a header that tries to send the request elsewhere, a body trying to be code, an odd '
     + 'method. The shape rules do not apply there, because somebody publishing a message '
     + 'legitimately sends any body at all; what counts instead is arriving with no name and being '
     + 'refused for it. The Authorization value is not written down, only whether there was one.'],
    ['|', 'Suricata, where it is running'],
    ['suricata_watch', 'Read what Suricata saw',
     'Suricata watches the packets arriving at the server in front \u2014 which this house never '
     + 'sees \u2014 and writes what it recognises to eve.json. A scan that never becomes a request, '
     + 'a handshake with something wrong in it, an exploit it knows by name. It does the recognising; '
     + 'the keeping out is still done here, by the same weighing and the same ladder, so nothing is '
     + 'refused for a reason nobody can read afterwards. Alerts about what this house SENT are not '
     + 'weighed against anybody.'],
    ['suricata_log', 'Where its eve.json is',
     'Usually /var/log/suricata/eve.json. It is one JSON object per line and gets big, so rotate it '
     + '\u2014 Suricata ships a logrotate file of its own.'],
    ['suricata_tls', 'Weigh the TLS fingerprints too',
     'A JA3 or JA4 fingerprint is the shape of a client\u2019s hello \u2014 the ciphers it offers, in '
     + 'the order it offers them \u2014 and it is the same for every copy of the same tool. So a '
     + 'hundred addresses with one fingerprint between them is one tool on a hundred machines, which '
     + 'no counter that watches addresses one at a time can ever see. It is weighed lightly, three '
     + 'apiece on all of them at once: a phone app, a browser and a Go program each have a '
     + 'fingerprint too, and a popular one is shared by thousands of people who have done nothing. '
     + 'Needs tls turned on in eve-log \u2014 see deploy/suricata.yaml, where it sits switched off '
     + 'with a note.'],
    ['tls_faces_bar', 'One address wearing this many fingerprints (a minute)',
     'The other way round, and the stronger of the two. A client has one hello and uses it: a browser, '
     + 'a phone app, a Go program each connect a thousand times wearing the same fingerprint. An '
     + 'address that opens twenty connections in a second wearing fifteen different ones \u2014 TLS '
     + '1.1 through 1.3, cipher list after cipher list \u2014 is a tool measuring what this server '
     + 'will accept, and it shows in a minute where its behaviour on the road would take an hour. '
     + 'Six is a fair start; a browser that has two or three over a minute is nothing at all.'],
    ['tls_spread_bar', 'One fingerprint from this many addresses (an hour)',
     'How many different addresses have to share a fingerprint within an hour before any of them is '
     + 'weighed for it. Ten is a fair start. Lower it and a popular browser version will eventually '
     + 'catch everybody using it; raise it and only a real spread shows.'],
    ['suricata_rules', 'Where its rules file is',
     'Usually /var/lib/suricata/rules/suricata.rules. Asked one rule at a time, when a sheet wants to '
     + 'say what a rule actually looks for \u2014 grepped for the one line rather than read, and the '
     + 'answer kept, since a rule does not change between fetches. It turns \u201crule 2034647\u201d '
     + 'into the rule\u2019s own words, what its authors classed it as, the strings it matches on, '
     + 'and whatever references they put on it. Empty and the sheet shows the number alone.'],
    ['suricata_least', 'How sure it has to be (1\u20133)',
     'Suricata says how sure it is about each thing it saw. 1 is \u201cthis is an attack\u201d and '
     + 'weighs eight; 2 is \u201cthis looks like one\u201d and weighs four; 3 is \u201cworth '
     + 'knowing\u201d and weighs one. This is the least sure it may be and still be weighed at all: '
     + '2 is the sensible answer, 1 if you want only what it is certain about, 3 if you want to see '
     + 'everything for a while. Classes that say plainly nothing is wrong are never weighed, at any '
     + 'setting.'],
    ['|', 'ntfy\u2019s own two files'],
    ['ntfy_own_watch', 'And ntfy\u2019s own two files',
     'Not nginx logs at all: the notification server\u2019s own log, and the ban feed it appends every '
     + 'address it has refused often enough to judge. Every line of the ban feed is an address ntfy has '
     + 'already decided about, so each one weighs a great deal here; its own log is read for the lines '
     + 'that name a visitor and complain, and its chatter about how many messages are cached says '
     + 'nothing about anybody and is ignored.'],
    ['ntfy_own_log', 'Its own log',
     'Where ntfy writes its own, e.g. /var/lib/ntfy/ntfy.log. It has to be a file it can write: the '
     + 'unit runs as the ntfy user, so /var/lib/ntfy is its own ground and /var/log is not.'],
    ['ntfy_ban_log', 'And its ban feed',
     'The file named by ban-file in /etc/ntfy/server.yml, e.g. /var/lib/ntfy/ban.log. ntfy appends a '
     + 'line to it whenever an address has been refused often enough in its window \u2014 time, '
     + 'address, prefix, HTTP code, ntfy code. Rotate it, or it grows for ever.'],
    ['|', 'how often all of it is read'],
    ['proxy_every', 'How often to read it',
     'How long to wait between readings, in minutes. Every minute is what it comes as; a log that '
     + 'nobody is writing to costs one very small SSH login a minute, and a busy one is read from '
     + 'where the last reading stopped, so more often is cheaper rather than dearer.'],
    ['proxy_watch', 'Read it by itself',
     'Read it and weigh what is in it. A path that is not there counts 2, something refused 3, more asking than it allows 3 \u2014 the same counting everything else goes through, so what it finds ends on the same list'],
  ]],
  ['The router that carries the list', [
    ['|', 'the machine'],
    ['mikrotik_host', 'Mikrotik: the router\u2019s address, so the blocked ones are dropped before they arrive. Empty means the router is not used at all'],
    ['mikrotik_port', 'Mikrotik: the SSH port'],
    ['mikrotik_user', 'Mikrotik: the user it logs in as \u2014 give it write access to firewall address lists and nothing else'],
    ['mikrotik_auth', 'Mikrotik: how it logs in'],
    ['mikrotik_password', 'Mikrotik: the password, when it logs in with one'],
    ['|', 'the list it carries'],
    ['mikrotik_list', 'Mikrotik: the address list it works with \u2014 the same name in both /ip and /ipv6, since the router keeps those apart and each address goes where it belongs. Nothing outside this one list is ever read or written, and inside it only the entries this put there \u2014 every one carries a comment starting zigmon:, and nothing without it is ever removed'],
    ['mikrotik_sync', 'Keep the router in step',
     'Mikrotik: keep the list on the router in step by itself. A change here reaches the router at once \u2014 an address blocked is a rule on the router a moment later, without waiting for anything'],
    ['mikrotik_every', 'Mikrotik: and look again how often, for blocks that ran out and for anything somebody changed on the router itself. The looking is cheap: one read, and nothing written unless the two disagree'],
    ['fold_versions_keep', 'Fold into subnets: how many earlier folds are kept, each one undoable on its own. Undoing one takes apart only what it made \u2014 anything kept out since stays kept out'],
  ]],
  ['Charts', [
    ['|', 'what the charts show'],
    ['chart_outages', 'Shade real outages'],
    ['link_plugs', 'Chart icon on plugs'],
    ['link_sensors', 'Chart icon on sensors'],
    ['link_buttons', 'Chart icon on buttons'],
  ]],
  ['Signing in', [
    ['|', 'who may see the house, and how long a sign-in lasts'],
    ['public_view', 'The house shows itself without a sign-in'],
    ['session_idle_min', 'A sign-in not used ends after (min)'],
    ['session_hours', 'A sign-in lasts at most (hours)'],
    ['login_tries', 'Wrong passwords before an address waits'],
    ['login_wait_min', '...and how long it waits (min)'],
  ]],
  ['Changes kept on trial', [
    ['|', 'a change that could cut the house off goes back by itself unless it is kept'],
    ['net_confirm_min', 'A new network goes back unless kept within (min)',
     'Counted from the moment the machine is back up and has its new address. 0: kept at once, with nothing to fall back on'],
    ['fw_confirm_min', 'A change of the firewall goes back unless kept within (min)',
     'The firewall and who may reach the pages. 0: kept at once'],
  ]],
  // the way out to the web through an HTTP proxy (5.35): otp's own words, for one machine
  ['Going out through a proxy', [
    ['webproxy', 'Go out through a proxy',
     'An HTTP proxy for what zigmon asks of the web: the new versions, the certificates from the ACME CA and its '
     + 'DNS APIs, the forecast, the lists, Shodan, Duo, ntfy and Telegram, the system updates. The house itself '
     + '(the plugs, Zigbee2MQTT, the broker, the SMS gateway, the router, the server in front, the Wi-Fi controller) '
     + 'and mail never use it, nor anything at an address of this network'],
    ['webproxy_url', 'The proxy', '<code>http://proxy.example.cz:3128</code>'],
    ['webproxy_user', 'User name at the proxy', 'Where the proxy asks for one. Empty: none.'],
    ['webproxy_password', 'Password at the proxy', 'Kept like the other passwords - never shown again, never written down.'],
    ['webproxy_no', 'Straight, not through the proxy',
     'Names and addresses reached without it, separated by commas: <code>.example.cz</code> is every name under it, '
     + '<code>10.0.0.0/8</code> a net. Addresses of this network go straight anyway.'],
    ['webproxy_os', 'The system updates through it too', 'dnf fetches the updates of System updates through it.'],
  ]],
  ['Restarting by itself', [
    ['|', 'a regular restart of the machine'],
    ['reboot_auto', 'Restart the server by itself'],
    ['reboot_at', 'At'],
    ['reboot_day', 'On (weekly)'],
  ]],
  ['Backups', [
    ['|', 'the nightly backup'],
    ['backup_enabled', 'Daily backup'],
    ['backup_time', 'Backup time'],
    ['backup_keep', 'Keep (count)'],
    ['backup_zigbee', 'Include Zigbee2MQTT'],
    ['full_backup_nightly', 'Also the whole installation'],
    ['full_backup_keep', 'Whole backups kept'],
  ]],
  ['Looking for new versions', [
    ['|', 'where new versions come from'],
    ['upgrade_check', 'Look for a new version every day',
     'Look for a new version every day — at the address below. A newer one is offered under From the web '
     + 'and said once, as A new version among the alerts; it is never put in by itself. Look now asks once '
     + 'whether this is on or off'],
    ['upgrade_url', 'Where new versions are looked for',
     'Where new versions are looked for — https:// only. A GitHub folder (github.com/OWNER/REPO/tree/BRANCH/FOLDER), '
     + 'a folder listing of a web server of your own, or one package’s own address. Whatever it is, a package '
     + 'is taken only when it is signed by a release key installed with zigmon'],
    ['downgrade_show', 'Offer going back to an older version',
     'Shows the card Going back to an older version, and lets the dashboard, zigmon --downgrade VERSION and '
     + 'the console do it. Off, none of them does; root on the command line can still go back with a package '
     + 'file (sudo zigmon --downgrade FILE). Going back leaves the database as it is, and only where that is safe'],
  ]],
  // the updates of the system on a schedule (5.34): otp's own words, for one machine
  ['On a schedule', [
    ['|', 'a look for updates every day'],
    ['osup_check', 'Look for updates every day',
     'dnf is asked what it has waiting, every day at the time below - for the card above, the alerts and the '
     + 'daily summary; nothing is put in'],
    ['osup_check_at', 'At', 'When, as <code>HH:MM</code> on this machine’s clock.'],
    ['|', 'the updates put in by themselves'],
    ['osup_auto', 'Updates on a schedule',
     'The updates put in by themselves in the window below - once on their day, within three hours of the time; '
     + 'one that cannot start then (an upgrade, a restart, a backup going on) is said in the events, not tried over and over'],
    ['osup_auto_what', 'Which', 'Only the security updates (<code>dnf --security</code>), or every update.'],
    ['osup_auto_every', 'How often', 'A day, a week or a month (on the 1st).'],
    ['osup_auto_day', 'On the day', 'The day a weekly update goes.'],
    ['osup_auto_at', 'At', 'When, as <code>HH:MM</code> - a quiet hour, away from the backups and the regular restart.'],
    ['osup_auto_reboot', 'After the update',
     'Where an update needs a restart - restarted by itself, as Restart and power off does it, or the restart offered here.'],
  ]],
  // Automatic certificates (5.30): otp's own words, for one machine
  ['Settings', [
    ['acme', 'Certificates from an ACME CA',
     'The certificate ordered from the CA and renewed by itself - by HTTP-01 (port 80 of the name open to the CA) '
     + 'or DNS-01 (a TXT record in DNS, nothing open to the internet). A certificate of your own (uploaded, answered '
     + 'to a request) is kept until <i>Look after it</i> or <i>Order it now</i> above'],
    ['acme_email', 'Mail of the account at the CA', 'Where the CA writes when a certificate runs out unrenewed. Empty: none'],
    ['acme_ca', 'The CA',
     'Let’s Encrypt, its staging (certificates browsers do not trust - to try it), or another ACME CA: step-ca, an internal one'],
    ['acme_url', 'Its ACME directory', 'The directory of another ACME CA, <code>https://ca.example.cz/acme/acme/directory</code>'],
    ['acme_ca_pem', 'The CA’s own certificate', 'The CA’s root (PEM), where the system does not trust it already'],
    ['acme_name', 'The certificate’s DNS name',
     'The name the certificate is for - its subject (CN) and its alternative name (SAN). Empty: the name nginx answers '
     + 'to (server_name), else the name of the certificate it has now, else the machine’s full host name. '
     + '<i>Its name…</i> above changes it too'],
    ['acme_days', 'Renewed when fewer days are left than',
     'Let’s Encrypt’s certificates last 90 days; 30 is what it advises. A certificate that lasts less is renewed at a third of its life'],
    ['acme_key', 'The key of the certificate',
     'Let\u2019s Encrypt takes RSA keys of 2048, 3072 or 4096 bits and ECDSA keys on P-256 or P-384. '
     + 'Both - what Let\u2019s Encrypt advises - orders two certificates for the name: RSA served by default, '
     + 'the much smaller ECDSA one to the clients that say they take it. The broker reads the RSA one'],
    ['acme_account_key', 'The key of the ACME account',
     'The key the account with the CA signs with - never one of a certificate. '
     + 'RSA 2048, 3072, 4096 or ECDSA P-256, P-384. A change makes a new account at the next order'],
    ['acme_challenge', 'How the CA checks the name',
     'HTTP-01 needs port 80 of the name reachable from the internet (or from the CA, for a CA of your own): root '
     + 'gives port 80 a location for <code>/.well-known/acme-challenge/</code>. DNS-01 needs nothing open: the CA reads a TXT record'],
    ['|', 'DNS-01'],
    ['acme_dns', 'Who puts the TXT record in DNS', 'By hand the order waits up to an hour for you; the others renew by themselves'],
    ['acme_dns_wait', 'Wait for DNS to carry it (s)', 'How long the record is given to reach every DNS server before the CA is asked'],
    ['acme_dns_server', 'The DNS server taking the updates',
     'The primary DNS server of the zone, <code>ns1.example.cz</code> or with a port <code>ns1.example.cz:5353</code>'],
    ['acme_dns_zone', 'The zone', 'Where the record goes, <code>example.cz</code>. Empty: as the server finds it'],
    ['acme_dns_tsig_name', 'The name of the TSIG key',
     'As the DNS server knows it, <code>zigmon-acme.</code>; allowed to update _acme-challenge records only'],
    ['acme_dns_tsig_alg', 'Its algorithm', 'As the key was made (tsig-keygen makes hmac-sha256)'],
    ['acme_dns_tsig_secret', 'The TSIG secret',
